[quote="Yoshi348"]

...I experimented on Tachyon rather than the Sentinels, and I feel like it paid off in her big burst-fueled cards. Really, though, this team is ridiculous....

[/quote]

Played a practice match to get the feel of it, and yep, this team is ridiculous; the combination possibilities are endless. &nbsp;Re: Experiment, 32 out of the 40 cards (80%) in the Sentinels deck are one-shots, so Experimenting on them with SS Tachyon is about as guaranteed a double-card-play as you&#39;re going to get. &nbsp;With the potential to use Experiment 3-4 times a round to fish for Team Communication and Unique Capabilities, you can get out all of their Signatures/Ongoings in no time flat. &nbsp;

&nbsp;

UPDATE: &nbsp;Mint. &nbsp;The God of Random Seeding was against me, so Experiment didn&#39;t work as well on the Sentinels&#39; deck as it did on my practice game, but still no sweat. &nbsp;Once Human Shield and Sentinels Tactics are out, the Ennead&#39;s piddly little instances of 1-3 AOE damage can&#39;t hurt the heroes, and all you need to watch out for are Ancient Magicks and Mass Overheating.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

P.S. &nbsp;This team was so ridiculously fun that I used them to take on Challenge Mode Spite and then Challenge Mode Voss. &nbsp;Again, once Human Shield and Sentinels Tactics came out, neither of them had a chance. &nbsp;Spite couldn&#39;t do any damage, so his Challenge condition (allowing him to heal beyond his base HP) was essentially a nullity. &nbsp;As for Voss, he has a plethora of minions that all do various kinds of 2-3 AOE damage, so after taking a few hits, the heroes were essentially invulnerable from him. &nbsp;Flipped him once and then kept killing his minions keeping him from ever flipping again, making his Challenge condition (playing an additional card when he flips) also useless.
